      "answer": "The hotṛ is the officiant who invokes and offers in the sacrifice, \"named by office rather than as an individual\" [E5]. The sacrifice (yajña) is \"the sacrificial rite itself, the act of worship offered to the gods with fire, speech and oblation\" [E6]. In the Atharvavedic passages retrieved, the hotṛ (or Agni identified as hotṛ) performs the invocatory speech that drives the rite: at a birth rite Aryaman as hotṛ utters the sacrificial call *váṣaṭ* [E1], and Agni as hotṛ, \"knowing the ways,\" bespeaks the births of the gods [E2].\n\nThe corpus distributions give the following per-corpus verse counts for the two concepts. For priest (hotṛ) [E7]: AV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 18, ABOUT_CONCEPT 21; RV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 195, ABOUT_CONCEPT 208; SV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 38, ABOUT_CONCEPT 41; YV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 49, ABOUT_CONCEPT 50. For sacrifice (yajña) [E8]: AV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 180, ABOUT_CONCEPT 176; RV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 473, ABOUT_CONCEPT 470; SV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 70, ABOUT_CONCEPT 70; YV — MENTIONS_ENTITY 116, ABOUT_CONCEPT 116. The evidence establishes the hotṛ's role as the invoking/offering priest within the sacrificial act, but does not elaborate further on the structural or procedural relationship between the two beyond these characterisations."
    }
